   \code{date} \tab  \tab \code{eq}, \code{ne}, \code{lt}, \code{le}, \code{gt}, \code{ge}, \code{between} \cr
   \code{datetime} \tab \code{tz} \tab \code{eq}, \code{ne}, \code{lt}, \code{le}, \code{gt}, \code{ge}, \code{between} \cr
   \code{int64} \tab  \tab \code{eq}, \code{between} \cr
   \code{duration} \tab \code{units} \tab \code{eq}, \code{ne}, \code{lt}, \code{le}, \code{gt}, \code{ge}, \code{between} \cr
   \code{list}, \code{other} \tab  \tab None \cr
}


Every column type also accepts \code{is_na} when a valid missingness mask is
available. A client decides what it can do with a column from \code{type},
\code{filterable}, \code{sortable}, and the grid \code{capabilities} alone. The schema's
optional \code{native_type} field is a producer-defined array of display strings.
A client may show those strings as a hint but must not infer semantics from
them. How R vectors map to protocol types is described, as informative text,
in the R column mapping section of
\link[=jdv_query_spec]{query specification}, and can change without a
protocol version change.

A client that receives a \code{type} it does not know must treat the column as
display-only, exactly like \code{other}: it follows the column's \code{sortable} for
sorting, offers no value-based filter, and uses only \code{is_na}. It must not
fail, hide the column, or guess from \code{native_type}. Because of this rule, a
new \code{type} can be added without changing the protocol major version. For the
same reason, a later release may add operators or operand forms to a known
type; a client offers only the operators it knows for that type and must be
prepared for the server to answer a request with an \code{unsupported} error,
which is an ordinary failure of that request and not a protocol violation.

 Column payloads:

Column identity is its zero-based \code{position}; \code{name} is display metadata and
may be empty, duplicated, or otherwise unusual. A schema entry includes
\code{position}, \code{name}, \code{type}, \code{sortable}, and \code{filterable}; \code{native_type} is
optional and, when present, is a producer-defined array of display strings.
Categorical schemas also include \code{levels} and \code{ordered}; datetime schemas
include \code{tz}; duration schemas include \code{units}. Unknown optional schema
fields must be ignored by clients so future schema extensions remain
compatible; the Column types section defines how to treat an unknown \code{type}
value.

A column payload is columnar. Channels may be omitted when unavailable:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{
  "values": [1, null, null],
  "display": ["1", "NaN", "NA"],
  "special": \{"nan": [1]\},
  "na_rows": [1, 2]
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{values}, \code{display}, and \code{sort_key}, when present, have one entry for each
row in the payload. \code{na_rows} lists missing row positions in this response,
starting at zero, in strictly increasing order without duplicates. Positions
must be integers within the payload's row count; they are not \code{source_row}
identifiers. \code{na_rows} is sent whenever missingness extraction succeeds,
including \verb{[]} when no rows are missing. Omission means missingness is
unavailable. It is authoritative; clients must not infer missingness from
display text. A producer may omit an unavailable channel while keeping other
channels usable. Clients use descriptor capabilities and channel availability
to decide which operations are available.

\code{special}, when present, is an object with only \code{nan}, \code{pos_inf}, and
\code{neg_inf} keys, in that producer order. Each value is a nonempty array of row
positions with the same integer, order, uniqueness, and range requirements as
\code{na_rows}. Empty kinds are omitted, and the whole field is omitted when no
kind occurs. Kinds must not overlap. NaN positions also occur in \code{na_rows}
when missingness is available, following R's \code{is.na()} semantics; infinities
are not missing. Clients reject invalid position channels as
\code{schema_changed}. The old \code{na} field and per-row \code{special} arrays are not
part of this format.

In snapshot access, \code{data.columns} entries contain the complete channel data.
A random-access response wraps each selected column as \verb{\{ "schema": ..., "data": ..., "errors": ... \}}, preserving the page-time schema. The \code{data}
object inside that wrapper uses the same channel format. Random-access rows
do not include \code{sort_key}; sorting remains on the producer side.

The following encoding rules apply:
\itemize{
\item \code{boolean}, \code{integer}, and finite \code{number} values use JSON booleans or numbers.
\code{duration} values are numbers in the schema's \code{units} (\code{secs}, \code{mins},
\code{hours}, \code{days}, or \code{weeks}), with the same missing and non-finite rules as
\code{number}; \code{display} carries the formatted text.
Missing values use JSON \code{null} and are identified by \code{na_rows}.
\item Non-finite doubles use JSON \code{null} in \code{values}; \code{special} identifies NaN
and positive or negative infinity by row position. NA is represented only
by \code{na_rows}. Numeric comparisons and sorting restore infinities from
\code{special}; \code{na_rows} controls missingness and NA-last placement.
\item \code{int64} values are canonical base-10 JSON strings, never JSON numbers, to
preserve exact integer precision. \code{na_rows} identifies missing entries. The
\code{display} channel may be omitted only when its nonmissing labels equal
\code{values} and every missing label is \code{"NA"}. The client reconstructs display
from values, using \code{"NA"} for missing entries. A producer may retain
\code{display} when its display text does not meet this rule.
\item \code{string} and \code{categorical} values are strings. The \code{display} channel may be omitted
under the same equality and missing-label rule. Categorical order and labels come
from \code{levels} and \code{ordered} in the schema.
\item \code{date} values are ISO \code{YYYY-MM-DD} strings. \code{datetime} values are ISO
timestamp strings; the schema's \code{tz} records the source time-zone setting.
\item In snapshot access, \code{sort_key} is present when a value's ordering cannot be
recovered from its \code{values} and \code{special} entries. It carries the producer's ordering
key; consumers compare keys rather than recomputing a producer's native
ordering. A \code{null} sort key is ordered after every non-null key, regardless
of \code{na_rows}. random-access rows never include sort keys.
\item \code{source_row} always reports zero-based positions in original source order.
Snapshot \code{data} and random-access page \code{data} may contain one \code{row_labels}
string per row; an absent or \code{null} field means there are no explicit row
labels. Grid descriptors do not contain \code{row_labels}.
}

Snapshot-access payloads contain all table rows in one \code{data} object.
Random-access payloads contain only the requested row slice. The protocol
guarantees matching sort and filter semantics, not identical display strings.

 Filter and sort request shape:

The filter is the neutral AST defined by
\link[=jdv_query_spec]{query specification}, not a UI library's filter model.
Objects contain exactly the fields required by their operator. Sort is an
array of objects with exactly \code{col} and \code{desc}; \code{desc} is a required scalar
Boolean. Duplicate sort and page columns are invalid. Cross-consumer examples
of filters, values, and expected original row indices are in the fixture
described in the \link[=jdv_query_spec]{query specification}.

Numeric filter special values use an explicit object, never a non-JSON
number:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{ "op": "eq", "col": 0, "value": \{ "special": "pos_inf" \} \}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{int64} filter values are canonical decimal strings. Missing and special
value semantics follow the \link[=jdv_query_spec]{query specification}.

 Errors and limits:

Error codes are stable machine-readable values. The \code{error} object contains
\code{code} and \code{message}, with optional \code{details}. The current codes include:

A consumer treats an unrecognized code as a general producer failure and may
display the code without requiring code-specific recovery behavior.\tabular{ll}{
   Code \tab Meaning \cr
   \code{invalid_request} \tab Malformed JSON, missing required fields, strict filter/sort field errors, or invalid parameters. \cr
   \code{unsupported} \tab A known operation or encoding is not supported for this table or protocol version. \cr
   \code{limit_exceeded} \tab A configured request, page, or JSON byte limit was exceeded. \cr
   \code{schema_changed} \tab The active view schema no longer matches its descriptor, or a required channel is unavailable. \cr
   \code{producer_error} \tab A producer method or other request computation failed. \cr
   \code{interrupted} \tab Request processing began and was interrupted. \cr
   \code{cancelled} \tab The accepted request was cancelled before processing began. \cr
   \code{expired} \tab The request deadline elapsed before processing began. \cr
   \code{unavailable} \tab A well-formed object path or resource node no longer resolves or cannot be read safely. \cr
   \code{stale_generation} \tab The request names a generation that is no longer active. \cr
   \code{view_not_found} \tab The view id is missing or closed. \cr
   \code{cache_changed} \tab A host-only sequential append cursor differs from the observed tail; refresh the host page catalog before advancing. \cr
   \code{in_use} \tab A session id is already active, another browser owns the sequential lease, a next request is still pending, or a reentrant operation cannot proceed. \cr
   \code{stream_failed} \tab A sequential producer encountered a terminal failure; close or register a new stream to continue. \cr
   \code{method_not_found} \tab No implementation exists for the requested method. \cr
}


Request and response size limits are negotiated in \code{hello}. A producer that
cannot satisfy a request or configured budget returns \code{limit_exceeded}.
Per-column channel failures may be reported in the page payload without
failing the entire request.

 Resource trees:

A resource tree lists the resources a session offers, such as the top-level
variables of an interpreter or the catalogs, schemas, and tables of a
database connection, so a viewer can browse them without opening a view
first. It is optional: a producer declares it with
\code{hello.params.capabilities.resources: true}. A host that does not use
resource trees ignores the capability and is not required to handle any
method in this section.

The resource tree belongs to the session, not to a view. It has no view id
and no generation, it is available while the session has no views, and
closing, refreshing, or replacing views does not change it. It ends with the
session. Inside a view, an object is explored with that view's own methods; a
resource tree only lists entry points and opens them as ordinary views.\tabular{llll}{
   Method \tab Direction \tab Kind \tab Result \cr
   \code{resources/children} \tab Host to producer \tab Request \tab One page of the children of a node or the root. \cr
   \code{resources/open} \tab Host to producer \tab Request \tab Opens one node as a new view. \cr
}


A host sends these requests only to a session whose \code{hello} declared the
capability. A producer that did not declare it returns \code{unsupported} or
\code{method_not_found}.
\subsection{Nodes}{

The root has no id and is never listed as a node. Every other node is
identified by its \code{id}, a non-empty string of at most 16,384 UTF-8 bytes
chosen by the producer. An id is opaque: a host or viewer stores it and sends
it back, but does not parse, build, compare as a path, or display it. An id
identifies a node within its session; it is not valid in another session.
While a node exists, the producer gives it the same id in every listing, and
it never gives that id to a different node. Whether a resource that is
removed and created again is the same node is up to the producer; for
example, a producer that identifies variables by name treats them as the
same. An id names a resource, not a value captured when the page was listed:
the producer resolves it again for every request, so a node that still exists
may describe or open different content after a change.

A child entry has these fields:\tabular{llll}{
   Field \tab Type \tab Required \tab Meaning \cr
   \code{id} \tab string \tab See text \tab Opaque node id. \cr
   \code{label} \tab string \tab Yes \tab Display name, at most 1,000 Unicode code points. \cr
   \code{kind} \tab string \tab Yes \tab Display hint token described below. \cr
   \code{summary} \tab string \tab No \tab Short description, at most 1,000 Unicode code points. \cr
   \code{has_children} \tab Boolean \tab Yes \tab Whether \code{resources/children} may list children of this node. \cr
   \code{viewable} \tab Boolean \tab Yes \tab Whether \code{resources/open} may open this node. \cr
   \code{hidden} \tab Boolean \tab No \tab Whether a viewer should hide this node by default; omitted means \code{false}. \cr
   \code{unavailable} \tab string \tab No \tab Token explaining why the node cannot be read, expanded, or opened. \cr
}


\code{has_children} and \code{viewable} are independent: a node may be both expandable
and viewable, either one, or neither. When neither is true, the node is still
listed so that the viewer shows it exists. \code{id} is required unless the node
cannot be identified within the id limit; such a node has
\code{has_children: false}, \code{viewable: false}, and \code{unavailable: "id_limit"}.

\code{kind} is a hint for icons and grouping and does not decide which view kind
opens. Recommended tokens are \code{container} (holds other resources, such as a
schema), \code{table} (rectangular data), \code{object} (structured data), \code{value} (a
scalar or simple vector), \code{function}, and \code{unknown}. More tokens may be
added; a viewer treats an unrecognized token as \code{unknown}. The kind of view
that opens is known only from the \code{view/open} descriptor. A label is not
unique and is not an id.

\code{kind} and \code{unavailable} tokens are 1 to 64 characters from lowercase ASCII
letters, digits, and \verb{_}.

\code{hidden} marks a node that the producer's environment conventionally hides,
for example a name with a leading dot in R. Producers still list hidden
nodes, and they can be addressed and opened as usual; whether to show them is
a viewer option. Hidden nodes count toward \code{offset}, \code{limit},
\code{total_children}, and \code{next_offset} like any other node, so a page may hold
only hidden nodes and still have a next page; a viewer advances by
\code{next_offset}, not by the number of rows it shows. \code{unavailable} is a short
token from a producer-defined vocabulary; a viewer may show it but must not
depend on a particular value, except \code{id_limit} above.

Producers may add fields to a child entry. A viewer ignores fields it does
not know.
}

\subsection{\code{resources/children}}{

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{
  "id": "resources-1",
  "method": "resources/children",
  "params": \{ "node": null, "offset": 0, "limit": 200 \}
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{node} is a node id, or \code{null} or omitted for the root. \code{offset} is a
zero-based, non-negative safe integer that defaults to 0; \code{limit} is an
integer from 1 through 500 that defaults to 200. Other values return
\code{invalid_request}. The result is:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{
  "node": null,
  "offset": 0,
  "children": [
    \{
      "id": "t:main.orders",
      "label": "orders",
      "kind": "table",
      "summary": "table, 12 columns",
      "has_children": false,
      "viewable": true
    \}
  ],
  "total_children": 1,
  "next_offset": null
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

All five fields are required. \code{node} and \code{offset} repeat the request, with
\code{null} for the root; \code{offset} is not clamped to the end. \code{children} is an
array of at most \code{limit} entries. \code{total_children} is the number of children
when the request was handled as a non-negative safe integer, or \code{null} when
the producer does not know it. \code{next_offset} is \code{null} when this page reaches
the end; otherwise it is \code{offset} plus the number of children returned, and
\code{children} is not empty. A producer may return fewer children than \code{limit} to
stay within its response byte limit, so a short page does not mean the end;
only \code{next_offset: null} does. If not even one child fits, the request fails
with \code{limit_exceeded} rather than returning an empty page that is not the
end. An \code{offset} at or past the end, and a node whose \code{has_children} is
false, return an empty page with \code{next_offset: null}.

Children come in an order that stays the same while the listed resources do
not change. The protocol does not otherwise define the order; a producer
extension may. A page reflects the resources when it was handled: if
resources are added or removed between page requests, a later page may repeat
or skip entries, and \code{total_children} may change. A viewer that appends pages
for one parent drops entries whose id it already added for that parent since
the listing last started from offset 0; entries without an id are never
treated as duplicates. The same node may appear under different parents. A
viewer refreshes by requesting again from offset 0, either on request or
after a change notification (see Change notifications below).
}

\subsection{\code{resources/open}}{

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{
  "id": "resources-2",
  "method": "resources/open",
  "params": \{ "node": "t:main.orders" \}
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{node} is a required node id. A producer extension may accept additional open
options. The producer resolves the node and opens it as a new top-level view,
sending its \code{view/open} notification with \code{reason: "resource"} before the
response. The result is that view's \code{view/open.params} descriptor; as for
\code{object/open}, a host registers the view from the notification, must not
create or revive a view from the response, and uses the response only to find
the view. If building the response fails, the producer closes the view and
does not send its \code{view/open}.

The opened view is a top-level view and has no parent view. It follows the
usual view lifecycle and descriptor capabilities, including the producer's
view limits. Listing or refreshing the resource tree does not affect any
view, and closing a view does not change the resource tree. Opening a node
registers a view like any other, so when the producer is at its view limit it
may close an existing view to make room, and \code{view/closed} is sent as usual.
Every successful open creates a new view, even for a node that is already
open.

Opening is not idempotent. After a host cancels a sent \code{resources/open} or
its deadline expires, the view may still be opened and its \code{view/open} may
still arrive. A host does not retry an open automatically.
}

\subsection{Errors}{

An id that is malformed or that the producer does not recognize as one of its
own ids returns \code{invalid_request}. A well-formed id whose resource no longer
exists or cannot be read safely returns \code{unavailable}, as does
\code{resources/open} on a node that is not viewable. A producer need not remember
the ids it has issued.

Resource tree requests follow the same request, queue, byte, acknowledgement,
wake, deadline, and cancellation rules as view requests. Because they name no
view, closing a view or moving it to a new generation does not cancel them;
they end with the requesting client or the session. Their responses are JSON
only and never carry an Arrow IPC binary body.
}

\subsection{Change notifications}{

A producer that declares \code{capabilities.resource_changes: true} (together with
\code{resources: true}) can tell a subscribed host that its resource tree may have
changed. The capability only makes the methods below available; declaring it
does not start any monitoring or send any notification.\tabular{llll}{
   Method \tab Direction \tab Kind \tab Result \cr
   \code{resources/subscribe} \tab Host to producer \tab Request \tab \verb{\{"enabled": <Boolean>\}}, the new state. \cr
   \code{resources/changed} \tab Producer to host \tab Notification \tab None. \cr
}


A session starts unsubscribed. \code{resources/subscribe} takes a required Boolean
\code{enabled} and sets the subscription of the session; repeating the same value
has no further effect. The result reports the state that now applies. When
enabling, the producer records the state it compares against before it
answers, so a change made after the response is reported. A host that wants
automatic updates therefore subscribes first and lists the tree after the
subscription succeeds. A producer that did not declare the capability returns
\code{unsupported} or \code{method_not_found}. The subscription belongs to the session:
the producer has a single host, which decides how to combine the wishes of
its own clients. It ends with the session.

While subscribed, the producer sends

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{ "method": "resources/changed", "params": \{\} \}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

when the listed resources may have changed. It is a hint that any listing the
host holds may be out of date, not a description of a change: it names no
node, several changes can be reported by one notification, and a notification
may arrive when nothing visible changed. A host refreshes the parts of the
tree it shows, as for a manual refresh, and lists hidden or collapsed parts
again when they are next shown. A producer that detects a change within the
scope its extension documents, while the session is subscribed and its
notifications can be delivered, sends at least one notification after that
change within a bounded delay. While an earlier \code{resources/changed} has not
yet been delivered, the producer may drop a new one, because the earlier one
already covers it. It sends no notification while unsubscribed. Unknown
\code{params} members are ignored. Viewers keep a manual refresh, because a
producer may not detect every change.
}
}

\section{R producer extension}{
 Resource trees:

The R producer declares \code{capabilities.resources: true} in \code{hello} by default
and serves the common resource tree with the variables of the global
environment. Set \code{options(jdv.resources = FALSE)} to turn it off: the option
is read when the session's \code{hello} is created and the result holds for the
whole session, so changing it later takes effect for the next session. A
session without the capability omits the \code{capabilities} member and answers
\code{resources/children} and \code{resources/open} with \code{unsupported}, without
revealing any name or summary. The same rule applies to the built-in
transport and to an embedded session started by \code{\link[=jdv_register]{jdv_register()}}. The tree is
available as soon as the session exists, also before any view is open.
\code{\link[=jdv_handle_request]{jdv_handle_request()}} does not accept \verb{resources/*} requests and returns
\code{method_not_found}.

The root lists the bindings of the global environment, one level only. The
search path and the contents of environments are not listed; an environment
is opened as a view and explored with \code{object/children}. Every child has
\code{has_children: false}. Bindings whose name starts with a dot are listed with
\code{hidden: true}. Children are ordered by name in the byte order of their UTF-8
form, independent of the locale; for example, \code{"Z"} comes before \code{"a"}.
Because the global environment changes between requests, the weak consistency
of the common protocol applies to paging.

An id names a binding, not its value. It is \verb{b:} followed by the UTF-8 name,
or \verb{h:} followed by the lowercase hex of the bytes of a name that is not
valid UTF-8. A viewer must still treat it as opaque. A name whose id would
exceed 16,384 UTF-8 bytes is listed without an \code{id}, with \code{viewable: false}
and \code{unavailable: "id_limit"}. An id with an unknown form returns
\code{invalid_request}; a binding that has been removed returns \code{unavailable}, for
both \code{resources/children} and \code{resources/open}.\tabular{ll}{
   \code{kind} \tab Value \cr
   \code{table} \tab A data frame, or an atomic matrix. \cr
   \code{function} \tab A closure, builtin, or special. \cr
   \code{value} \tab Any other atomic vector, including a factor. \cr
   \code{object} \tab Everything else, such as a list, an environment, \code{NULL}, or an S4 object. \cr
   \code{unknown} \tab A binding that cannot be read safely, or whose name has no id. \cr
}


The kind is decided from the type and the class attribute alone. No
\code{jdv_source()} method or other S3 method is called to choose it, so the view
that finally opens is known only from its \code{view/open} descriptor. The R
extension fields \code{class} and \code{type} have the same meaning as in
\code{object/children}. \code{summary} is at most 1,000 code points and is built
without calling any S3 or S4 method defined for the value, because the root
is listed without the user choosing individual objects: it never reads
elements, calls \code{format()}, or calls a generic such as \code{length()}, \code{dim()},
\code{names()}, or \code{[}. It is the class (or type) name followed by the stored
size: \verb{data.frame [32 × 11]} (rows and columns), \verb{matrix double [3 × 4]},
\code{double (100)}, \code{factor (150)}, \code{list (5)}, \code{function}, \code{environment} (no
binding count), and the class name of an S4 object. A length is the stored
length of the object, not the result of a user-defined \code{length()} method. The
summary after a value is opened with \code{object/children} is unchanged.

A binding is read without evaluating it. Promises that have not been forced,
active bindings, and missing arguments are listed with \code{viewable: false} and
\code{unavailable} set to \code{delayed_binding}, \code{active_binding}, or
\code{missing_binding}, and \code{resources/open} returns \code{unavailable} for them;
neither request evaluates them. A forced promise is read as its value. Before
R 4.6.0 the value of a binding cannot be read without evaluation, so the
names are listed with \code{kind: "unknown"}, \code{viewable: false}, and
\code{unavailable: "opaque_binding"} (or \code{active_binding} for an active binding),
and \code{resources/open} returns \code{unavailable}.

\code{resources/open} accepts \code{node} and the optional \code{mode} and \code{collation} of
\code{object/open}, with the same values and errors. It opens the current value of
the binding as a new top-level view with \code{reason: "resource"}. The view's
\code{title} is the variable name written as an R symbol (backquoted when it is
not syntactic). It is a display name only and takes no title slot, so it
never replaces another view, even one opened with \code{\link[=jdv_view]{jdv_view()}} under the same
title, and opening the same variable again gives another view with the same
title. The \code{view/open} notification is queued before the response, and the
view is closed if the response cannot be built. Opening counts against
\code{jdv.max_tables} like any other registration. From R 4.6.0 the view follows
the binding as \code{\link[=jdv_view]{jdv_view()}} does: the descriptor carries \code{tracking}, refresh
reads the current value, and a reassigned variable updates the view. A value
that is a sequential stream, or an R version before 4.6.0, gives a view
without tracking. Listing never closes a view, and opening may close the
oldest view when \code{jdv.max_tables} is reached.

A children page that does not fit \code{jdv.max_message_bytes} is shortened to
fewer children, with \code{next_offset} pointing at the first child left out. A
single child that does not fit returns \code{limit_exceeded}.
\subsection{Change notifications}{

The R producer declares \code{capabilities.resource_changes: true} together with
\code{resources: true}, so a session with the resource tree always offers
\code{resources/subscribe}; \code{options(jdv.resources = FALSE)} removes both. A
session with the resource tree turned off answers \code{resources/subscribe} with
\code{unsupported}, and \code{\link[=jdv_handle_request]{jdv_handle_request()}} returns \code{method_not_found} for it.
Declaring the capability starts no monitoring: a session is unsubscribed
until the host subscribes, and the subscription, with everything kept for it,
ends with the session (a disconnect, a notification queue overflow, or
unloading the package). \code{enabled} must be a Boolean, otherwise the request is
\code{invalid_request}; other members are ignored.

While subscribed, R compares the listing with the one it saw last. It
compares what the root shows for each binding: the name and id, \code{kind},
\code{class}, \code{type}, \code{summary}, \code{hidden}, \code{unavailable}, and \code{viewable}. A
binding that is added, removed, or listed differently, such as a new class,
other dimensions, or a promise that was forced and can now be opened, makes R
queue \code{resources/changed}. Before R 4.6.0 the comparison still sees names and
whether a binding is an active binding. Because the listing does not show
scalar values, a reassignment that is displayed the same, such as \code{x <- 1}
followed by \code{x <- 2}, is not reported; a view opened from the variable is
kept current by tracking, and opening always reads the current value. R keeps
only this display metadata, not the values, and reads bindings the way the
listing does: it does not evaluate a promise or an active binding and does
not call an S3 or S4 method of a value.

R looks after each top-level task has completed, and also at each call of
\code{jdv_rpc(NULL)}, whether the task succeeded or not. A change inside a
function, or in the middle of a long task such as \code{Sys.sleep()}, is seen only
after the task ends. R does not look while a request or refresh is running or
the transport is busy; the next look covers it. The cost is proportional to
the number of bindings in the global environment: every task of a subscribed
session lists all of them, whatever the page size, and there is no strict
time limit.

At most one \code{resources/changed} waits in the notification queue. While one
has not been taken, by the transport that sends the queue or by
\code{\link[=jdv_notifications]{jdv_notifications()}}, further changes queue nothing, and other notifications
are never merged or dropped. After it is taken, the next change queues a new
one. Hosts that use the tree still refresh manually, because a change that
the listing does not show is not reported.

The same task callback serves tracked views and the subscription, and each
keeps its own state: it exists while a view is tracked or the session is
subscribed. Closing the last tracked view keeps watching for a subscribed
session, and unsubscribing keeps tracking views.
}

 \code{object/children}:

This method is available only on tree views. A grid with \code{children: true}
uses \code{object/open} to capture a cell; it does not expose \code{object/children}.

Parameters are \code{view}, \code{generation}, \code{path}, and optionally \code{offset},
\code{limit}, and \code{attributes}. The path is a full typed path from the root value
held by the named tree view; an empty path selects that root. \code{offset} is
zero-based and defaults to 0; \code{limit} defaults to 200 and must be between 1
and 500. \code{attributes} is a Boolean that defaults to \code{true}; any other value,
including \code{null}, returns \code{invalid_request}. A path contains at most 32
steps. Each step is one of:\tabular{lll}{
   Step \code{type} \tab Fields \tab Meaning \cr
   \code{index} \tab \code{index} \tab One-based position in an unnamed or positional object. \cr
   \code{binding} \tab \code{name} \tab Environment binding name. \cr
   \code{slot} \tab \code{name} \tab S4 slot name. \cr
   \code{attr} \tab \code{name} \tab Attribute name. \cr
}


The result contains the same \code{view}, \code{generation}, full \code{path}, and \code{offset},
plus \code{children} and \code{total_children}. Each child has its one-based \code{index},
bounded \code{label}, string-array \code{class}, \code{type}, bounded \code{summary},
\code{has_children}, \code{viewable}, and its full typed \code{path} when the child is
addressable. \code{class} and \code{type} have the same meaning as in a tree
descriptor; a child that is unavailable has an empty \code{class} and no \code{type}. A
child whose binding, slot, or attribute name exceeds 1,000 characters is not
addressable: it has \code{viewable: false}, \code{has_children: false},
\code{unavailable: "path_limit"}, and may omit \code{path}. Other children include the
full typed path. When a value cannot safely be read, the child carries a
bounded \code{unavailable} reason. Child text, each class string, and each summary
are at most 1,000 characters. Summaries inspect at most five values or
children and at most three levels deep. Environment names are enumerated
locally and paged; active and delayed bindings are reported as unavailable
without reading their values.

Children are the elements of the object followed by its attributes. Within an
atomic vector (logical, integer, double, complex, character, or raw), every
element is a leaf with \code{has_children: false}. Its \code{summary} has the form
\verb{<class> (1): <value>}, for example \code{double (1): 1.5}, and always includes
the value regardless of depth. A page of elements is read and formatted as
one range, so only the requested range of an ALTREP vector is read.
Dimensionless atomic vectors with a class, excluding S4 objects, use the
column display format for the requested range; if that formatting fails,
their underlying atomic values are shown instead. A missing value is shown as
\code{NA}, and strings and formatted values are cut at 160 characters. A factor
element, and every value in a factor's summary, is shown as its level name; a
missing code is \code{NA}, and a code outside the levels is shown as
\verb{<invalid code N>}. An atomic vector has children only if it has more than
one element, or if attributes are listed and it has any, so a length-one
vector without attributes is a leaf.

A list object with a class-specific \code{format} method shows its logical value
count as \verb{(N values)} in the summary, using the class's \code{length} method; one
value is written \verb{(1 value)}. The summary may preview up to five formatted
values using the class's subset method and the column display format; this
preview does not change the count. A zero length shows \verb{(0 values)} without
calling subset or format. If \code{length} fails or returns an invalid value, the
summary shows the class without a count. If subset or display fails, the
valid count remains. At the summary depth limit, only the class is shown,
with no count, and \code{length} and \code{format} are not called. \code{POSIXlt} and
classed vctrs records use this rule. Plain lists, data frames, and \code{AsIs}
lists retain their existing summaries. Attributes can change \code{total_children}
without changing this logical value count. This formatting applies only to
summaries; listing children does not dispatch list subset methods.

With \code{attributes: false}, the attribute children (\code{attr} steps) are left out:
of every vector and other object, including a vector's \code{names}, which remains
visible as the label of each element. \code{total_children}, paging, and
\code{has_children} of the listed children follow the setting, and attributes that
are present are neither counted nor paged. Environment bindings and S4 slots
are the contents of those objects, not attribute children, so the setting
does not hide them.

Tree navigation never evaluates a promise, active binding, classed \code{[[}, \code{$},
or \code{names} method. Paths are resolved again for each \code{object/children}
request.

 \code{object/open}:

Parameters are \code{view}, \code{generation}, a non-empty \code{path}, and optional \code{mode}
and \code{collation}. \code{mode} must be exactly \code{"auto"}, \code{"snapshot"}, or \code{"paged"};
when omitted, it defaults to \code{"auto"}. \code{collation} must be exactly \code{"C"} or
\code{"locale"}; when omitted, it uses the same \code{getOption("jdv.collation", "C")}
default as \code{\link[=jdv_register]{jdv_register()}}. These options apply when the selected value
opens as a grid and do not inherit from the parent view. If the selected
value opens as a tree, valid \code{mode} and \code{collation} values are ignored.
Invalid values, including explicit \code{null}, return \code{invalid_request}.
\code{object/children} does not accept these options.

On a tree view, every path step uses the \code{type} discriminant from the table
above. On a grid view, the first step must select a drillable cell:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{
  "view": "v-1",
  "generation": 1,
  "path": [\{ "kind": "cell", "source_row": 204, "column": 2 \}],
  "mode": "paged",
  "collation": "C"
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{source_row} is the original, zero-based source-row position, independent of
sorting, filtering, and pagination. \code{column} is the zero-based schema
position, including when column names are duplicated. \code{kind: "cell"} is
allowed only at the start of a grid path. Remaining steps, if any, use the
tree \code{type} forms above and are resolved within the captured cell value. The
complete path, including its cell step, contains at most 32 steps. Grid
\code{object/open} requires \code{capabilities.children: true} and
\verb{columns[column].drillable: true}; malformed selectors or non-drillable
columns return \code{invalid_request}.

Each grid column schema includes the Boolean \code{drillable}, which says whether
the column's cells can be selected by \code{kind: "cell"}. This metadata is
independent of \code{type}, \code{sortable}, and \code{filterable}; clients must not infer
drillability from the type, \code{native_type}, or display text. The R columns
that are drillable are listed in the R column mapping (informative) section
of \link[=jdv_query_spec]{query specification}. Snapshot views retain the R
source only when a drillable column needs subsequent cell capture. Retained
source references are released when the view closes.

\code{object/open} resolves the selected value once, holding that captured value
in the new view. A path that cannot be resolved returns \code{unavailable};
malformed paths return \code{invalid_request}, missing views return
\code{view_not_found}, and old generations return \code{stale_generation}. The result
is the child's \code{view/open.params} descriptor, but the child is registered by
the \code{view/open} notification that precedes the response (see the Notification
delivery section); a host must not create or revive a view from the response,
and may use it only to find the child. If building the response fails, the
child is closed and no \code{view/open} for it is sent. The child descriptor has
\code{reason: "child"}. If the parent has a \code{title}, the child's \code{title} is an R
expression built from the parent's \code{title} and the opened path; if the parent
has none, the child has none either. A list element uses \code{x$name} when its
name is nonempty and unique and \code{x$`a b`} when the name is not a
syntactic name, and \code{x[[3]]} (one-based) otherwise. An element of a named
atomic vector uses \code{x[["name"]]} under the same rule. An S4 slot is \code{x@slot},
an environment binding is \code{x$name}, an attribute is \code{attr(x, "names")}, and a
grid cell is \code{df[12, "col"]} with the original zero-based \code{source_row} 11, or
\code{df[12, 3]} with a one-based column position when the column name is missing,
empty, or duplicated. A child of a child continues from the child's \code{title},
for example \code{x$a[[2]]@data}. The \code{title} is truncated to 4,096 bytes. It is a
display label only: it is not guaranteed to be valid R, a host must not
evaluate it, and it does not identify the view or take a title slot. A tree
child starts a new root with an empty path; the descriptor may identify its
parent with \code{parent}. The limit is 32 live descendant views total per root,
counting nested tree and grid views across all levels. Closing a child
releases its slot for a later child view. Closing, replacing, or evicting a
parent closes its descendants; each cascaded child emits \code{view/closed} with
\code{reason: "parent_closed"}.

The browser uses the same child-view navigation for tree and grid parents.
Opening controls appear only in drillable cells and use the displayed row's
original \code{source_row} (shown as a one-based row number). The tree viewer
shows the root \code{class}, and a checkbox, checked by default, sets the
\code{attributes} parameter of its \code{object/children} requests. View breadcrumbs
restore the parent's sort, filter, page, horizontal scroll, or tree path and
child-page offset. A pending child open is tied to the active parent view and
generation, separately from page query epochs: Snapshot sort, filter, and
page operations remain local and do not cancel the captured cell request.
Returning to the R prompt lets that original request complete without another
browser request. Selecting a different view, replacing its generation, or
closing it cancels its pending browser requests. Display text and navigation
labels are inserted as DOM text.

R 4.6 and newer use the public experimental \code{R_GetBindingType()} and
\code{R_getVarEx()} APIs to distinguish \code{Value}, \code{Forced}, \code{Delayed}, \code{Unbound},
\code{Missing}, and \code{Active} bindings. Only \code{Value} and \code{Forced} bindings are
read; \code{Delayed}, \code{Unbound}, \code{Missing}, and \code{Active} bindings are opaque, and
delayed expressions are never deparsed. On older R versions only active
bindings can be identified; other environment bindings are treated as opaque.
For ALTREP values, bounded element or region access may call the class's
representation callbacks to read values needed for a page or summary. This
does not force delayed promises or invoke active bindings. Tree access does
not request \code{DATAPTR}; the protocol does not guarantee total memory use by an
ALTREP implementation. If reading an individual child fails, that child is
returned with an \code{unavailable} reason and sibling children remain available.
S4 \code{.Data} is opaque; slots are inspected as stored values.

 \code{view/refresh}:

The common request, capability gate, generation transition, notification, and
response contract is defined above. This section describes how the R producer
performs that operation.

The view is rebuilt from the value captured when it was registered, so the
rebuild shows the same data unless the captured object is a reference object
that changed in place. A tracked view (see Binding tracking) is instead
rebuilt from the current value of its variable, read without evaluation; it
fails with \code{unavailable} when tracking has ended (the reason is in the
message) or the variable is a promise or active binding. A view whose
tracking is \code{"paused"} can be refreshed too: it is rebuilt from the current
value of its variable, the new object becomes the baseline, and the state
stays \code{"paused"}. The rebuild happens even if nothing changed and always
advances the generation by one. The registration \code{mode} and \code{collation} are
reused as they were requested: an \code{"auto"} mode is decided again (a grid may
move between Snapshot and Paged, and the effective mode is never frozen), an
explicit mode is kept, and a \code{NULL} collation intent reads \code{jdv.collation}
again. A refresh of a titled view keeps its title association, so a later
registration with that title continues from the refreshed generation.

The replacement is built first. Only if the view, its generation, and (for a
child view) its parent are still current is it published, as one step, under
the same view id with the new generation, and the new \code{view/open} is queued.
Then every descendant view is closed, deepest first, with \code{view/closed}
notifications carrying \code{reason: "parent_closed"}. If releasing the old
generation closes the refreshed view, its \code{view/closed} follows the new
\code{view/open}, so the host never keeps a generation R no longer has. The old
generation of the refreshed view itself gets no \code{view/closed}; its
\code{view/open} successor replaces it. As for any request, these notifications
precede the response. A refresh that fails, is interrupted, or finds the view
stale leaves the generation and all children unchanged. A \code{\link[=jdv_close]{jdv_close()}} or
\code{\link[=jdv_disconnect]{jdv_disconnect()}} issued by user code while the replacement is being built
keeps its effect: the closed view is not revived and the request fails with
\code{view_not_found}. \code{\link[=jdv_register]{jdv_register()}}, \code{object/open}, or another refresh
attempted by user code during the rebuild fails with \code{in_use}. If the
notification queue overflows, the session ends as described under
Notification delivery.

Refreshing a child view keeps it a child of the same parent generation (the
descriptor repeats \code{parent}); it is refused with \code{view_not_found} once the
parent was refreshed, replaced, or closed. Sequential views are not
refreshable (\code{unsupported}) because a consumed stream cannot be rewound.
Errors are \code{invalid_request}, \code{view_not_found}, \code{stale_generation},
\code{unsupported}, and the usual \code{limit_exceeded}, \code{producer_error}, and
\code{interrupted}.

A hub that forwards requests treats a sent \code{view/refresh} specially. The
successful refresh queues the new generation's \code{view/open} before its own
response, and a hub normally discards pending requests of an older generation
when it sees a newer \code{view/open}. The response of a sent \code{view/refresh} is
not discarded for that reason, whether it reports success or failure, as long
as the request was not timed out, cancelled, or lost to a disconnect. The hub
checks that the response matches the request (same id, view, and requested
generation as \code{previous_generation}, and a generation one higher on success).
The \code{view/open} notifications alone update what is displayed; the response
only confirms the operation and must never move the display back to an older
generation.

 \code{view/track} and \code{view/tracking}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{"id": "track-1", "method": "view/track",
 "params": \{"view": "v-1", "generation": 3, "enabled": false\}\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}\tabular{lll}{
   Field \tab Request \tab Result \cr
   \code{view} \tab Required; the view id \tab The view id \cr
   \code{generation} \tab Required; must be the current generation \tab The generation after the request; one higher when rebuilt \cr
   \code{enabled} \tab Required boolean; \code{false} stops, \code{true} resumes \tab Omitted \cr
   \code{tracking} \tab Omitted \tab The \code{tracking} object after the request \cr
}


Other fields are rejected with \code{invalid_request}, as is a non-boolean
\code{enabled}.

\code{enabled: false} changes the state from \code{"active"} to \code{"paused"}. The binding
and the baseline object are kept, and the variable is not examined while the
view is paused. \code{enabled: true} changes \code{"paused"} back to \code{"active"}. At
that moment the current value of the variable is read and compared with the
baseline. If it differs, the view is rebuilt in the same way as an automatic
update: a new \code{view/open} with \code{reason: "tracked"}, \code{tracking.state: "active"}, and the next generation is queued, and the result carries the new
generation. If it is the same object, the generation does not change. A value
that cannot be read without evaluation (a promise or an active binding) is
looked at again later, as for any check. If the rebuild fails, a
\code{view/update_failed} is queued as for an automatic update and the view stays
active at the same generation. A request for the state the view already has
succeeds and changes nothing. If the variable turns out to be gone, the state
becomes \code{"lost"} (announced as below) and the result reports it.

A view that has no \code{tracking} field, or whose state is \code{"lost"}, fails with
\code{unsupported}, and the message names the reason. A generation that is not
current fails with \code{stale_generation}, and an unknown view with
\code{view_not_found}. The other errors are \code{invalid_request}, \code{producer_error},
and \code{interrupted}.

\code{view/tracking} is a notification:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{"method": "view/tracking",
 "params": \{"view": "v-1", "generation": 3,
            "tracking": \{"name": "df", "state": "lost",
                         "reason": "removed",
                         "message": "the binding was removed"\}\}\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

R queues it when the state changes without a new \code{view/open}: when following
is lost, and when \code{view/track} pauses, or resumes without rebuilding. When
\code{view/track} resumes and rebuilds, the new \code{view/open} carries the state and
no \code{view/tracking} is sent. \code{generation} is the generation the state belongs
to; a host ignores a notification for a generation it no longer shows, and it
updates its record of the view's \code{tracking} and \code{capabilities.track} (false
for \code{"lost"}). The notification precedes the response of the request that
caused it, like every notification. Following is lost when the variable is
removed or its environment is garbage collected, which R finds in the task
callback, in a \code{view/refresh}, or when \code{view/track} resumes.

A hub that forwards requests may treat a sent \code{view/track} like a sent
\code{view/refresh}: a successful rebuild raises the generation by one, and the
\code{view/open} alone updates the display.

 \code{view/close} and \code{view/closed}:

The common request, response, and notification contract is defined above. In
R, a successful \code{view/close} removes the entry from the active registry
immediately. If a request is already using its entry, source and holder
cleanup is deferred until that request releases it.

When the registry evicts its oldest active table to satisfy \code{jdv.max_tables},
it queues a \code{view/closed} notification with the evicted \code{view} and
\code{generation}. The notification enters the session queue ahead of the
associated new \code{view/open}, and the host delivers both in queue order.
\code{\link[=jdv_close]{jdv_close()}} queues \code{view/closed} for views it closes. Re-registering an
active title replaces the same id with a newer generation; after close or
eviction, a later registration uses a new id and generation 1.

Closing a tree view removes its child views recursively. A \code{view/closed}
notification for a child closed by its parent includes
\code{reason: "parent_closed"}; the explicitly closed root has no such reason.
Replacing or evicting a parent has the same descendant-close effect.

 R-specific descriptor and runtime details:

The R producer adds \code{class} and \code{type} to tree descriptors. \code{class} is a
string array following \code{\link[base:class]{base::class()}} semantics for the root object, and
\code{type} is the string returned by \code{\link[base:typeof]{base::typeof()}} (for example \code{"list"},
\code{"double"}, \code{"environment"}, or \code{"S4"}). Its tree descriptors use
\code{kind: "tree"}, \code{access: "random"}, \code{encoding: "jdv-json"}, and include a
\code{path} array that is empty at the root. Grid descriptors omit \code{class} and
\code{type}.

R may include a \code{tracking} object when a view follows a variable. It has the
form \verb{\{"name": "df", "state": "active"\}}: \code{name} is the variable name and
\code{state} is \code{"active"}, \code{"paused"}, or \code{"lost"}. The lost state includes a
\code{reason} token and optional \code{message}. A view that does not follow a variable
omits \code{tracking} and sets \code{capabilities.track} to false. The R producer sends
\code{reason} on every \code{view/open}; it sets the token according to the table in
the common descriptor section.

R column channel diagnostics use an empty \code{errors} array on random-access
pages when no channel failed, and a named object of channel-specific messages
when errors are present. Snapshot-access columns include \code{errors} only when a
channel failed. Missingness failures use the \code{na_rows} diagnostic key,
matching the wire channel name. Channel failures are reflected in the schema
capability flags; failed channel results are not cached.

The R package uses base R and its bundled cJSON implementation at runtime; it
does not require jsonlite. Consumers can parse and construct JSON with their
own tooling and pass a JSON string to \code{\link[=jdv_rpc]{jdv_rpc()}}. Inbound requests follow
the common parser limits above. Generated R messages are bounded by the
outbound byte limit and are not revalidated with the request parser's node or
object-member limits.

The R producer generates session ids as \verb{r-<pid>-<connection counter>} and
does not use or change R's random state. It identifies itself as jdv running
on R and sets \code{wake: true}. Its grid descriptors send all eight known
capability flags. The R API emits \code{hello} on explicit connect or with the
first registered view on an implicit connection; a disconnect releases the
process-wide view registry.

In the R local transport, \code{wake} checks whether queued requests can run, but
a wake does not dispatch work while R is in a function frame or at a
receive-only API preflight. A 50 ms interval is a valid host choice but is
not required by the protocol. A running R method is not forcibly interrupted
by \code{cancel}.

The cJSON writer first tries a 15-significant-digit representation for a
finite double and accepts it only when its own read-back is exactly equal to
the source; otherwise it falls back to 17 significant digits. The emitted
text round-trips to the source double with correctly rounded parsers.
Negative zero is written as \code{0}, without its sign.

The nanoarrow adapter obtains the schema during registration without
advancing the stream, then transfers stream ownership to the native layer.
Close, replacement, eviction, parent cascade, namespace unload, and producer
disconnection release the stream and any carried batch exactly once. A
release callback may execute R code; registry identity and generation are
checked again before publishing a result. Releases requested inside an active
producer callback are deferred until that callback finishes. The synchronous
\code{\link[=jdv_rpc]{jdv_rpc()}} API rejects \code{stream/next} before consuming the producer; the
local transport uses its private binary-capable entry point.

R producer budgets, page retries, and interruption behavior are described in
the following implementation notes.

Per-column channel failures are represented in the page payload and do not
necessarily fail the whole request. The request and response byte limits are
\code{jdv.max_request_bytes} (default 1 MiB, minimum 128 bytes) and
\code{jdv.max_message_bytes} (default 10 MiB, minimum 1,024 bytes). The JSON
output limit applies to final JSON text, not peak allocation; Arrow IPC
bodies use the separate binary-transfer limits above. For finite doubles, the
cJSON writer first tries a 15-significant-digit representation and accepts it
only when its own double read-back is exactly equal to the source value;
otherwise it falls back to 17 significant digits. It releases each completed
column tree while appending it to the output.

If a Paged response exceeds the output limit, the already-computed result is
retried as progressively shorter row prefixes; the R query and user methods
are not run again. If even one row cannot fit, \code{limit_exceeded} is returned
with a \code{warnings} array, which may be empty.

Full sort and filter operations have no work or row limit by default because
they are explicit user requests. An explicitly finite \code{jdv.max_work_bytes} or
\code{jdv.max_query_rows} caps that work. Interrupt delivery in the event-loop
path depends on the suspension state at callback entry: an interrupt caught
during a started request returns \code{interrupted}, while R may defer delivery if
the callback began with interrupts already suspended. The viewer UI should
show that R is busy while a request runs.

Snapshot construction, Paged registration probes, page extraction, and JSON
encoding use separate finite budgets. Each derived budget is the smaller of
\code{INT_MAX}, six times the applicable output allowance plus 4 MiB, and an
explicitly configured \code{jdv.max_work_bytes}. For Snapshot, the output
allowance is \code{min(jdv.snapshot_max_bytes, jdv.max_message_bytes)}; for page
work it is \code{jdv.max_message_bytes}. This preserves a 64 MiB work allowance
with the 10 MiB defaults while allowing Snapshot construction to scale with
its configured threshold. When \code{jdv.max_work_bytes} is finite, query work,
page extraction, and response encoding draw from the same per-operation
allowance. The cache budget limits the combined estimated bytes across
column, channel, and index caches; oversized entries are skipped and old
entries are evicted. The cache allowance is held by the registered table and
is separate from per-operation work. Unfiltered page requests do not
materialize a complete row index and are limited by page size.

These are conservative engine-work estimates, not process-memory or RSS
limits: source storage, arbitrary user method allocations, R allocator and GC
overhead, and transient R-internal string conversion or sorting scratch are
outside the guarantee. Snapshot checks raw source strings before formatting
and checks returned channel sizes before further encoding; Paged registration
avoids scanning the full source and charges requested pages and queries.
Oversized common metadata is rejected at registration, while explicit Paged
row names are checked only for requested rows. If Auto selects Snapshot but
full explicit row names exceed Snapshot's metadata budget, registration
retries as Paged; forced Snapshot registration returns \code{limit_exceeded}.
Packed matrix columns are summarized by shape for display when copying a wide
row would exceed the cell preview bound, and missingness scans are budgeted
before allocating masks. Minimal control and error responses have a separate
fixed 64 KiB encoder-work allowance, so a depleted request budget can still
report its failure.

 Registration API:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{jdv_register(
  x,
  title = NULL,
  mode = c("auto", "snapshot", "paged"),
  collation = NULL
)

jdv_rpc(request = NULL)

jdv_view(x, title = NULL, mode = c("auto", "snapshot", "paged"),
  collation = NULL)

jdv_close(id = NULL)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{x} may be a data frame, matrix, or another R object. Data frames and
matrices register as grid views. Arrow Table and RecordBatchReader objects,
\code{nanoarrow_array_stream} pointers, and Polars DataFrame objects register as
forward-only Arrow grid views through the optional nanoarrow producer
adapter. Their \code{mode} and \code{collation} arguments do not change sequential
access. Other objects use the tree view by default. \code{\link[=jdv_register]{jdv_register()}} returns
\code{list(id, generation)} and does not open a socket. Its \code{view/open} message,
and every other notification, is queued; see the Notification delivery
section.

\code{\link[=jdv_view]{jdv_view()}} registers the view, opens the local connection on first use
(unless \code{\link[=jdv_connect]{jdv_connect()}} already did), sends \code{hello} on a new connection, then
the queued notifications (\code{view/closed}, then \code{view/open}), and returns the
view id invisibly. The R API keeps the return-list key \code{id} and generates
opaque view id strings such as \code{v-1}; consumers must not interpret the id
format. Its default title is the deparsed expression passed as \code{x}; an
explicit \code{title = NULL} creates a new slot. \code{jdv_close(id)} closes one view,
and \code{\link[=jdv_close]{jdv_close()}} closes every view. Closing the last view stops a connection
that \code{\link[=jdv_view]{jdv_view()}} opened implicitly, after the close notification and any
pending response are sent; a persistent connection from \code{\link[=jdv_connect]{jdv_connect()}} stays
open until \code{\link[=jdv_disconnect]{jdv_disconnect()}}. \code{\link[=jdv_rpc]{jdv_rpc()}} with no argument or \code{NULL}
explicitly polls the active connection and drains at most eight requests.
This recovery path bypasses the normal event gate and is non-reentrant.
Passing a JSON string to \code{\link[=jdv_rpc]{jdv_rpc()}} remains a synchronous,
transport-independent embedding call.

The address is selected from \code{options(jdv.address = ...)}, then \code{JDV_ADDRESS},
then \code{discovery.json} under the user cache directory. On Linux and other Unix
systems, the directory is \verb{$XDG_CACHE_HOME/jdv} or \verb{~/.cache/jdv}; on macOS it
is \verb{~/Library/Caches/jdv}; on Windows it is \verb{\%LOCALAPPDATA\%/jdv} or
\verb{\%USERPROFILE\%/AppData/Local/jdv}. The discovery file is at most 64 KiB and
contains a string field named \code{socketPath}. Unix-domain socket paths may be raw
absolute paths or \verb{unix:///absolute/path}; Windows named-pipe addresses use
\verb{npipe:////./pipe/NAME}. A \verb{tcp://localhost:port}, \verb{tcp://127.0.0.1:port}, or
\verb{tcp://[::1]:port} address is accepted only when explicitly configured. TCP has
no authentication, and named-pipe ACLs are not hardened. Windows named pipes
must use byte-type mode; message-type pipes are unsupported.

Host support depends on the R front end's event loop and interrupt behavior.
Rgui, RStudio, R.app, Esc/Ctrl-C delivery, and true top-level behavior must
be validated in each target host. See \code{\link[=jdv_view]{jdv_view()}} for the local transport
execution gate.

A non-\code{NULL} title identifies one active display slot. Registering that
active title again replaces its contents, reuses its view id, and increments
its generation. The new view is prepared before the prior view is replaced,
so a failed registration leaves the prior registration intact. Closing or
evicting a title removes its title mapping; registering it later creates a
new id at generation 1. \code{NULL} titles always create independent
registrations. A new slot starts at generation 1. If \code{jdv.max_tables} is
lowered below the active count, registration evicts as many oldest entries as
needed to satisfy it.

Snapshot registration materializes and encodes its data in the \code{open}
message. After successful message construction, a Snapshot with drillable
columns retains its source and registration fingerprint in R for cell
inspection. Other Snapshots retain only table metadata. The host is
responsible for caching the snapshot data in both cases, and neither has a
\code{grid/rows} endpoint. Paged registration retains the source and its initial
schema in R and returns schema and dimensions in \code{open}; rows are computed on
demand.

For Paged tables, dimensions, column names, and column classes are checked
against the registration fingerprint on requests. Mutation of values through
a shared reference cannot be detected reliably. Register the table again
whenever values, schema, methods, or options that affect extracted channels
change. Re-registration creates a fresh holder and cache. Changing a class
method while continuing to use already cached channels does not guarantee
refresh.

The default collation is \code{getOption("jdv.collation", "C")}. Current options
include \code{jdv.max_message_bytes} (default 10 MiB, minimum 1,024),
\code{jdv.max_request_bytes} (default 1 MiB, minimum 128), \code{jdv.max_tables}
(default 64), \code{jdv.max_page_rows} (default 2,000), \code{jdv.snapshot_max_cells}
(default 10,000), \code{jdv.snapshot_max_bytes} (default 10 MiB),
\code{jdv.max_work_bytes} (default \code{Inf}), \code{jdv.max_cache_bytes} (default 32 MiB),
and \code{jdv.max_query_rows} (default \code{Inf}). \code{jdv.resources} (default \code{TRUE})
enables the resource tree for a session (see R producer extension: Resource
trees). The work and row limits apply to full sort/filter operations; setting
either option to a finite value restores the corresponding \code{limit_exceeded}
guard. Page, work, cache, and query-row settings are captured when a Paged
holder is registered; re-register the table to apply changed options. Each
open Paged view separately retains its latest filtered/sorted row index
outside \code{jdv.max_cache_bytes}, allowing consecutive pages for that query to
reuse the index. A different query or unfiltered page releases the
latest-index slot; an older result may remain in the LRU index cache.
Clearing query caches, replacing the view generation, or closing the view
releases both the slot and caches. This adds at most one potentially large
result index per open Paged view; the 32 MiB default remains the budget for
the LRU caches, including older result indices.

\code{mode = "auto"} selects Snapshot only when both the cell estimate
\code{nrow * max(1, ncol)} and a deterministic byte estimate fit their thresholds.
The byte threshold is \code{min(jdv.snapshot_max_bytes, jdv.max_message_bytes)}.
The cell estimate counts \code{source_row} even for a zero-column table. Byte
estimation samples at most 256 evenly spaced rows; snapshot construction then
checks actual source string lengths and generated channel sizes. A sampled
estimate can therefore miss a large value, in which case automatic mode
discards the incomplete Snapshot attempt and registers a Paged holder. Forced
Snapshot mode returns \code{limit_exceeded} instead.

 Binding tracking and \code{view/update_failed}:

\strong{Protocol contract.} A view that follows a variable carries a \code{tracking}
object in its \code{view/open} (see Message envelopes). While the state is
\code{"active"}, the view is rebuilt with \code{reason: "tracked"} and the next
generation whenever the variable holds a different object than the view was
built from, and that object becomes the new baseline. While the state is
\code{"paused"}, the variable is not examined, so changes do not rebuild the view.
When following ends, the state becomes \code{"lost"} with a \code{reason}, the last
view stays, and \code{view/tracking} announces it; a later \code{view/refresh} fails
with \code{unavailable}. The following sections describe how the R package
implements this.

\strong{R implementation.} A view can follow the variable it was created from.
\code{\link[=jdv_view]{jdv_view()}} does so by default when its argument is a single name, and
\code{\link[=jdv_register]{jdv_register()}} accepts an explicit \code{binding = list(name = "x", env = env)}
for embedding hosts. After each top-level R task, the R task callback reads
every tracked variable without evaluating it, compares the object it holds by
identity with the one the view was built from, and rebuilds every changed
view through the same update as \code{view/refresh}: a new \code{view/open} with the
next generation (and \code{parent_closed} closes of its children) is queued, and
the baseline moves to the new object. Nothing is scheduled beyond the task
callback; a check is skipped while a request or a refresh is running and made
at the next opportunity. When the built-in connection owns the session the
queue is sent right after the check.

Only plain and already forced bindings are read (R 4.6.0 or later). Promise,
active, and missing bindings are skipped and looked at again later. If the
variable is removed or its environment is garbage collected, tracking ends,
the last view stays, and a later \code{view/refresh} fails with \code{unavailable}; a
recreated variable is not picked up. Objects changed in place (environments,
reference objects, \code{data.table} updates by reference) keep their identity and
are not detected; a manual \code{view/refresh} covers them. The environment is
held weakly, but a displayed object that refers to it, such as a closure,
keeps it alive. Views opened from another view and sequential views are not
tracked. A paused view is skipped by the task callback, which exists only
while at least one view is active.

\strong{Embedding hosts.} R runs task callbacks in registration order. A host
whose own callback takes notifications before the callback of jdv would see
an update only after the next command. Such a host calls \code{jdv_rpc(NULL)}
first, which checks tracked variables once even without a viewer connection,
and then \code{\link[=jdv_notifications]{jdv_notifications()}}.

When an update cannot be built (a schema, conversion, or size limit, or an R
error), the previous generation stays and one notification is queued:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{"method": "view/update_failed",
 "params": \{"view": "v-1", "generation": 3, "code": "limit_exceeded",
            "message": "..."\}\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{generation} is the generation still displayed and \code{code} is one of the usual
error codes. The same object is not tried again, and so not reported again,
until the variable holds a different object or \code{view/refresh} is requested. A
host that forwards the notification ignores one for a generation it no longer
shows. A notification queue overflow ends the session as described under
Notification delivery.

 Registry-free R handles:

\code{\link[=jdv_handle]{jdv_handle()}} captures a data frame, matrix, or list for an embedding host
without registering a viewer slot. Handle operations do not consume
\code{jdv.max_tables}, advance viewer identifiers, evict views, or produce
notifications. \code{\link[=jdv_handle_register]{jdv_handle_register()}} is the exception: it registers the
captured value as an ordinary view, with the same registry limit, return
value, notification, and transport flush behavior as \code{\link[=jdv_register]{jdv_register()}}. It
uses the captured value directly and does not track a variable binding. The
resulting view has a lifetime independent of the handle. Handles work without
a session and alongside either an embedded session or a viewer connection.
Closing registered views or disconnecting does not close handles. Sequential
sources, including Arrow and Polars sources, are unsupported.

\code{\link[=jdv_handle_descriptor]{jdv_handle_descriptor()}} returns a JSON string with the shape of \code{view/open}
params (or the \code{object/open} result for a child). \code{\link[=jdv_handle_request]{jdv_handle_request()}}
accepts only \code{grid/rows} and \code{object/children}, with the same parameter
validation and result shapes as their viewer requests. It returns the result
itself as JSON, without a response envelope. Omit \code{view} and \code{generation}
from its R-list params: the handle supplies them, and specifying either is an
\code{invalid_request} error. Result identifiers are opaque handle identifiers,
separate from viewer identifiers, with generation \code{1}. They cannot be used
with \code{\link[=jdv_rpc]{jdv_rpc()}} or \code{\link[=jdv_close]{jdv_close()}}.

\code{\link[=jdv_handle_register]{jdv_handle_register()}} uses the handle's mode and collation intent when
those arguments are \code{NULL} (the default), and reuses its title unless a title
is supplied. A closed handle or parent signals \code{view_not_found}, an operation
on a busy handle signals \code{in_use}, and a changed table schema signals
\code{schema_changed}.

\code{\link[=jdv_handle_open]{jdv_handle_open()}} takes a typed path and returns a child R handle; call
\code{\link[=jdv_handle_descriptor]{jdv_handle_descriptor()}} on that child to obtain its descriptor JSON. Other
methods passed to \code{\link[=jdv_handle_request]{jdv_handle_request()}}, including \code{object/open}, fail with
\code{method_not_found}. These functions add no wire methods or fields; there is
no \code{view/describe} method.

Use \code{mode = "paged"} for an initial preview bounded by rows, selected \code{cols},
and output bytes. The \code{limit} and \code{cols} params select the page; \code{max_bytes}
bounds the UTF-8 JSON result. Oversized row or child pages shrink by halving
their returned item count. A single item that cannot fit fails with
\code{limit_exceeded}. Descriptors are never truncated. Auto mode retains the
usual Snapshot selection, and Snapshot descriptors contain the complete
captured data and do not support \code{grid/rows}.

Handles do not follow variable bindings or support refresh or tracking; their
descriptor capabilities report both as false. Table handles retain the
captured source, including in Snapshot mode, and check its dimensions, column
names, and column classes against the initial fingerprint. This does not
detect every value-only mutation through a shared reference or changes to
methods. Close and recreate a handle when those change.

\code{\link[=jdv_handle_close]{jdv_handle_close()}} closes the handle and all its descendants, is
idempotent, and emits no notifications. Subsequent use fails with
\code{view_not_found}. A live child retains its parent so that garbage collection
of a parent variable does not invalidate the child. Explicit parent close
still invalidates descendants. Unreachable handles release their resources
through finalizers. Handles belong to the current R session and are not
transferable between processes. See \code{\link[=jdv_handle]{jdv_handle()}} for examples and limits.

 Notification delivery:

Every notification R produces for a session (\code{hello}, \code{view/open},
\code{view/closed}, including evictions, cascaded child closes, the \code{view/open} of
a child created by \code{object/open}, \code{view/update_failed}, and \code{view/tracking})
goes through one first-in, first-out queue. A session has exactly one owner
of that queue, reported by the \code{owner} field of \code{\link[=jdv_connection]{jdv_connection()}}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"transport"}: the built-in connection, whenever one is open. The
transport sends the queue itself, before the response of the request being
handled, so a notification always precedes the response that caused it.
\item \code{"embedded"}: a session started by \code{\link[=jdv_register]{jdv_register()}} when no connection
exists. \code{hello} is queued before the first \code{view/open}. The host takes the
queue with \code{\link[=jdv_notifications]{jdv_notifications()}}, which returns the JSON messages in order.
The session remains after its last view closes so the final \code{view/closed}
can be taken, and ends with \code{\link[=jdv_disconnect]{jdv_disconnect()}}.
}

Ownership never moves implicitly: \code{\link[=jdv_connect]{jdv_connect()}} and \code{\link[=jdv_view]{jdv_view()}} fail while
an embedded session exists, and \code{\link[=jdv_notifications]{jdv_notifications()}} fails while the
transport owns the queue. An embedding host delivers every message taken by
\code{\link[=jdv_notifications]{jdv_notifications()}} in order, and delivers the string returned by
\code{\link[=jdv_rpc]{jdv_rpc()}} only after the notifications produced by that request. For
\code{object/open}, the child's \code{view/open} is in the queue when \code{\link[=jdv_rpc]{jdv_rpc()}}
returns, so the host registers the child from that notification and uses the
response only to find it. \code{\link[=jdv_rpc]{jdv_rpc()}} never embeds notifications in its
response.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{id <- jdv_register(mtcars)$id
deliver(jdv_notifications())         # hello, view/open
response <- jdv_rpc(request_json)    # a request received from the viewer
deliver(jdv_notifications())         # notifications caused by the request
deliver(response)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The queue is bounded by \code{jdv.notification_max_messages} (default
\code{max(256, 4 * jdv.max_tables)}) and \code{jdv.notification_max_bytes} (default
\code{4 * jdv.max_message_bytes}), both read when the session starts. Messages are
never dropped silently: when adding one would exceed a limit, the session
ends, every view is closed, the queue is discarded, and
\code{last_disconnect_reason} of \code{\link[=jdv_connection]{jdv_connection()}} becomes
\code{"notification_overflow"}. The operation that overflowed fails with an error
of class \code{jdv_notification_overflow}. With the built-in transport the queue
is normally sent right away, so an overflow mostly affects an embedded host
that does not take its notifications. A host must treat the end of a session,
seen as an error from R or as \code{last_disconnect_reason}, as the loss of every
view.

 Local transport and execution gate:

The following limits and dispatch rules describe the R producer transport;
another producer may implement its execution gate differently. The R
transport bounds the pending queue to 256 messages and 16 MiB by default.
Each inbound JSON line is limited by \code{jdv.max_request_bytes} (1 MiB by
default), and each outbound line by \code{jdv.max_message_bytes} (10 MiB by
default). A read callback processes at most \verb{max(64 KiB, request limit + 1)}
bytes. One native poll or event callback drains at most eight queued
requests; each request in that batch passes through its own execution-gate
check. Sends have a one-second timeout. Limits can be adjusted with
\code{jdv.transport.queue_messages}, \code{jdv.transport.queue_bytes},
\code{jdv.transport.max_drain_requests}, and \code{jdv.transport.send_timeout_ms}.

The event source reads and validates framed input into a bounded C queue. It
does not leave a readable descriptor active after hitting its receive quota.
If the pending queue is full by message count or bytes, the transport returns
\code{limit_exceeded} for that request alone and keeps the connection and already
queued work when the error response is sent successfully. A failed error send
still disconnects the host and closes registered tables. Each receive
callback is also bounded to
\code{jdv.transport.queue_messages + jdv.transport.max_drain_requests} frames and
\verb{max(64 KiB, request limit + 1)} bytes. \code{cancel} and \code{wake} notifications
count toward the frame limit. A host that exceeds either callback quota is
disconnected. Each receive callback acknowledges the cumulative number of
complete frames read, including malformed or rejected frames; the host
releases frame and byte credit through that boundary. Since the R producer
sets \code{hello.params.wake} to \code{true}, hosts must send \code{wake} notifications
repeatedly at a regular interval while a request awaits a terminal response.
A single wake is insufficient for this R execution gate: a normal package
API-entry preflight is receive-only and does not dispatch work, and receiving
a wake while R is in a function frame only allows requests to be queued for
later dispatch. A 50 ms interval is a valid implementation choice, but the
protocol does not require every host to use that interval. Wake frames remain
subject to the same applicable credit and rate limits as other traffic. Hosts
must keep at most 32 view requests awaiting a response. Before sending a page
request that makes an unsent page request obsolete, hosts must replace the
unsent request with the newer page. Hosts must bound the cumulative
unconfirmed output prefix to the receive callback's frame and byte quotas so
multiple writes cannot exceed one callback's budget when coalesced. Each
\code{cancel} and \code{wake} frame consumes the same frame and byte credit as a
request. Before dispatch, the transport removes cancelled and expired queued
requests and sends their terminal errors, including while another R method is
running. The R adapter returns \code{view_not_found} for a closed or missing view
and \code{stale_generation} for an obsolete generation. For each request, dispatch
requires native \code{busy == 0} and a single \code{\link[base:sys.nframe]{base::sys.nframe()}} evaluation in
\code{R_BaseEnv} to return zero. If the queue is empty, the gate skips that
evaluation. A started request that fails, including an interrupt, is terminal
and is never retried. A synchronous or queued stale generation returns
\code{stale_generation}. A second live pending request with the same typed id is
rejected as \code{invalid_request} on a best-effort basis and closes the
connection to avoid ambiguous responses. Cancelled requests no longer reserve
their ids.

The gate temporarily saves \code{R_interrupts_suspended}, sets it to \code{TRUE} for
the single frame-count evaluation, and restores the prior value with
unwind-safe cleanup. It does not use \code{END_SUSPEND_INTERRUPTS}, call \code{onintr},
or inspect or change private pending-interrupt state. This practical
function-frame check is not a guarantee that R is idle: on Windows, a timer
can run during a top-level \code{for} loop while \code{\link[base:sys.nframe]{base::sys.nframe()}} is zero, and
this dispatch behavior has been observed. The Windows timer interval is about
200 ms. If the request callback begins with interrupts enabled, an interrupt
after dequeue becomes an \code{interrupted} error. If R entered the callback with
interrupts already suspended, R may defer delivery; long sort or filter work
may therefore not stop promptly. The viewer should show a busy state and
offer a retry after a terminal failure.

\code{jdv_rpc(NULL)} is a non-reentrant recovery path that bypasses the normal
gate and drains at most the configured batch under \code{R_UnwindProtect}; it does
not use a background \code{R_ToplevelExec}. Disconnect cleanup is deferred until
the callback stack is safe. A public R \code{\link[base:addTaskCallback]{base::addTaskCallback()}} performs
cleanup after a successful top-level task, while explicit polling and later
public package entries also check for pending cleanup. This task callback is
cleanup-only and does not dispatch requests. It is an event-driven cleanup
opportunity, not a general timer. A disconnect closes every registered table,
and the next \code{\link[=jdv_view]{jdv_view()}} call starts a new connection; no in-flight request
is retried.

On namespace unload, the transport source is stopped, queued requests and
registry entries are closed, and the housekeeping callback is removed. If a
native callback is still active, the package namespace may unload while the
native library remains loaded while native calls are active; an idle reload
or unload can release it later. Forced DLL unload while native callbacks are
active is unsupported; reloading is rejected while the previous native owner
is still active.

R evaluation, condition handling, and interrupt state use installed public R
APIs. The POSIX input handler uses the installed \code{R_ext/eventloop.h} external
interface, which is not a stable API. No GPL R source or later implementation
is copied.
}

